title: Crypt::Age's own CSPRNG returns are unchecked, and one of them wraps our data
  key
updated: 2026-08-16T00:34:32Z
---

Found while fixing karr #44, which put a length check on the two random
values File::SOPS generates itself (the data key and the per-value GCM
nonce). That check stops at this distribution.

The age layer generates two more random values, in Crypt::Age::Primitives
(Crypt::Age 0.001), both from Crypt::PRNG::random_bytes and neither checked:

  generate_file_key   random_bytes(FILE_KEY_SIZE)   -- 16 bytes
  (nonce)             random_bytes(16)

plus a Crypt::PK::X25519 ephemeral key via generate_key.

The file key is what ChaCha20-Poly1305 encrypts our data key under, so a
short return there weakens the wrapping of the key that protects every value
in the document. As with #44, nothing downstream would notice: ChaCha20 keys
are a fixed size so a short one would fail loudly, but the 16-byte nonce has
the same property the GCM nonce had -- shorter is still accepted.

Not fixable here: Crypt::Age is a separate distribution. The options are an
upstream patch (Getty-authored, so cheap), or a check in
File::SOPS::Backend::Age on what comes back, which can only see the finished
stanza and not the values that went into it. Probably upstream, in the same
shape as the refs/cryptx-pv-defect.md report for karr #42.

Low priority: unlike #44 there is no unchecked read() involved, only
unchecked returns from a CSPRNG that has never been observed to return short.
#44 was raised because its fallback path made a short return plausible.
Closed by orchestrator during board hygiene: work shipped in a2ffd22 (Backend::Age::decrypt_data_key refuses a data key that is not 32 bytes) plus the encrypt-side _random_bytes checks. Pinned by t/21-random-bytes.t.
